  "worked_solution": "First we plot the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{10})}^{x}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/f02afa6dd2d1c107fd72b1221fddb7f6c698506deb834cad0dc11af1bebb18e6.png)\n\nNext, we shift this graph $\\frac{1}{10}$ units down. \nThe horizontal asymptote under such a transformation becomes $y=-\\frac{1}{10},$ the $y$-intercept becomes $(0,\\frac{9}{10}).$\nThe resulting graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{10})}^{x}-\\frac{1}{10}$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/57539870ff45bae449d3ad7c11580d9c8068cfe76ac98c2f849ee38494288036.png)",
